  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If $11fg+7=f+4g,$ then $g=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to isolate all terms with $g$ on one side of the equation. To do this, we start by subtracting $7$ from both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg+7 & =f+4g \\\\ 11fg+7-7 & =f+4g-7 \\\\ 11fg & =f+4g-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract $4g$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg & =f+4g-7 \\\\ 11fg-4g & =f+4g-7-4g \\\\ 11fg-4g & =f-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all terms containing $g$ are on the left-hand side, and all terms independent of $g$ are on the right-hand side.\n\nThen, we factor out $g$ in the expression on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11fg-4g & =f-7 \\\\ g(11f-4) & =f-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $(11f-4)$ to isolate $g:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(11f-4) & =f-7 \\\\ \\frac{g(11f-4)}{11f-4} & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4} \\\\ \\frac{g\\cancel{(11f-4)}}{\\cancel{11f-4}} & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4} \\\\ g & =\\frac{f-7}{11f-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $11f-4\\ne 0.$",
